WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.575
Match the accelerator to the memory and compute profile of the job.

00:00:03.575 --> 00:00:08.220
VRAM must hold model weights, context cache, activations, runtime overhead, and,

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.760
for training or fine-tuning, optimizer and gradient state.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.365
Model file size alone is not a complete requirement.

00:00:14.365 --> 00:00:16.655
Longer context, larger batches, vision inputs,

00:00:16.655 --> 00:00:20.400
and concurrent sequences can raise memory use after the model has loaded.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.450
GPU generation affects supported numerical formats, software compatibility,

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.460
and performance.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.690
Memory bandwidth influences how quickly large model weights can be moved during inference.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.800
Raw compute matters more for some training and image workloads.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.460
Review measurements from a comparable model and runtime rather than assuming that a higher

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.575
product number is always more economical.

00:00:38.575 --> 00:00:42.720
A single high-memory GPU is often easier to use than several smaller devices when one

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.155
model must fit as a unit.

00:00:44.155 --> 00:00:47.775
Multi-GPU instances can support model parallelism, larger training jobs,

00:00:47.775 --> 00:00:48.960
or multiple replicas,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.050
but the framework must distribute work effectively and interconnect performance can

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:53.805
matter.

00:00:53.805 --> 00:00:56.966
Several GPUs do not automatically create one shared memory pool.

00:00:56.966 --> 00:01:01.200
Inference usually prioritizes memory fit, latency, throughput, and serving efficiency.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.000
Fine-tuning may require much more memory and checkpoint storage.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.900
Quantization can make inference practical on a smaller rental,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.080
while parameter-efficient tuning can reduce training requirements.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.800
Vision and multimodal models add image-processing and memory demands.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.820
Test the smallest plausible configuration with real prompts, context, and batches,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.910
then move upward only when measurements identify a constraint.
